+section('docker', 'Docker registry integration', 'box-seam')
  p.
    A job can use Docker in two different ways. Keep them apart, because they log in and run differently:
  .row.g-3.mb-3
    .col-md-6
      .card.h-100
        .card-body.small
          h3.h6 #[code --docker-image]: the DUT
          p.mb-0.
            SW jobs only. The Client pulls the image and runs it as the job's #[strong DUT container]
            (an emulator the tests talk to), sandboxed, next to your command. The pull uses the
            #[strong Client host's] Docker login, made once by the service user.
    .col-md-6
      .card.h-100
        .card-body.small
          h3.h6 #[code docker …] inside #[code --command]
          p.mb-0.
            Any job type, on a host with Docker. Your command logs in, pulls and runs containers
            itself, e.g. to run the tests #[em inside] an image. Credentials come from the job
            (#[code --env]) and are scoped to the job.

  h3.h6 Log in to a registry on the Client host (for --docker-image)
  p.small.
    #[code --docker-image] is pulled with #[code docker pull] as the Client's service user, from the registry the reference names
    (Docker Hub for a short name such as #[code python:3.14]). Log in once per host, as that user:
  +code('Client host').
    # the user that runs thub-client (the one who ran sudo npm i -g), e.g. thub:
    sudo -u thub docker login registry.lab.local:5000
    sudo -u thub docker login ghcr.io -u &lt;github-user>        # password: a PAT with read:packages
    sudo -u thub docker pull registry.lab.local:5000/dut-emulator:2026.08   # check it works
  p.small.
    A plain-HTTP registry must be listed in the Docker daemon's #[code insecure-registries]:
  +code('/etc/docker/daemon.json').
    { "insecure-registries": ["registry.lab.local:5000"] }
  p.small.
    Then run #[code sudo systemctl restart docker]. An image that can't be pulled ends the job as #[code ERROR]
    during #[em prepare], naming the registry and Docker's reason. An image already on the host isn't pulled again.

  h3.h6 How the Client starts the DUT container
  p.small.
    You don't pass #[code docker run] parameters for the DUT. The Client always starts it like this (image default command,
    sandboxed), and removes it and its network when the job ends:
  +code('What the Client runs (equivalent)').
    docker pull registry.lab.local:5000/dut-emulator:2026.08
    docker network create --driver bridge thub-job-&lt;jobId>
    docker run -d --name thub-&lt;jobId> --network thub-job-&lt;jobId> \
      --memory 2g --cpus 2 --read-only \
      -v &lt;workDir>/&lt;jobId>/downloads:/downloads:ro \
      -p 127.0.0.1::5555 registry.lab.local:5000/dut-emulator:2026.08
  ul.small
    li The image must #[strong start the DUT by itself] (its #[code CMD]/#[code ENTRYPOINT]) and should listen on port #[strong 5555].
    li The job's #[code --download-file] files are mounted read-only at #[code /downloads] (e.g. the firmware to emulate).
    li The command reaches it at #[code $THUB_DUT_HOST] (#[code 127.0.0.1:&lt;port&gt;]) and by name as #[code $THUB_DUT_CONTAINER].
    li Its output is shipped as the #[code emulator] log stream. Its root filesystem is read-only: don't install or clone into it.
    li #[code thub run --dry-run] prints the exact docker commands a job would run, without running anything.

  h3.h6 Execute commands in the DUT container
  +code('SW job: talk to the DUT').
    thub run --type sw \
      --docker-image registry.lab.local:5000/dut-emulator:2026.08 \
      --download-file https://artifactory.example.com/fw-local/app/1.4.0-42/app.elf \
      --command 'docker exec "$THUB_DUT_CONTAINER" /opt/emu/status &&
                 docker logs "$THUB_DUT_CONTAINER" | tail -n 20 &&
                 ./ci/test.sh --dut "$THUB_DUT_HOST"' \
      --wait

  h3.h6 Log in and pull inside a job (credentials scoped to the job)
  p.small.
    Pass the credentials as #[code --env] (masked everywhere, dropped when the job ends). Set #[code DOCKER_CONFIG] to the
    job's work directory so the login is deleted with the workspace instead of staying in the service user's
    #[code ~/.docker] for every later job:
  +code('Any job type').
    export DOCKER_PASSWORD=…        # from your CI secret store, never on the command line
    thub run --type hw \
      --env DOCKER_REGISTRY=registry.lab.local:5000,DOCKER_USER=ci --env DOCKER_PASSWORD \
      --command 'export DOCKER_CONFIG="$THUB_WORK_DIR/.docker" &&
                 echo "$DOCKER_PASSWORD" | docker login "$DOCKER_REGISTRY" -u "$DOCKER_USER" --password-stdin &&
                 docker pull "$DOCKER_REGISTRY/team/test-runner:1.4"' \
      --wait

  h3.h6 Run the tests inside a container (docker run parameters)
  p.small.
    The command always starts on the Client host, in #[code $THUB_WORK_DIR] (the #[code --git-repo] checkout). Mount it,
    hand over the variables the tests need with #[code -e NAME], and use #[code --rm]:
  +code('Tests in an image').
    thub run --type sw \
      --env DOCKER_REGISTRY=registry.lab.local:5000,DOCKER_USER=ci --env DOCKER_PASSWORD \
      --env TARGET=staging \
      --git-repo git@github.com:yourorg/web-ui-tests.git main \
      --command 'export DOCKER_CONFIG="$THUB_WORK_DIR/.docker" &&
                 echo "$DOCKER_PASSWORD" | docker login "$DOCKER_REGISTRY" -u "$DOCKER_USER" --password-stdin &&
                 docker run --rm \
                   -v "$THUB_WORK_DIR:/work" -w /work \
                   -e TARGET -e THUB_SUITE -e THUB_JOB_ID \
                   --network host \
                   "$DOCKER_REGISTRY/python:3.14" ./run-tests.sh --junit results/junit.xml' \
      --wait
  table.table.table-sm.small
    thead
      tr
        th docker run option
        th Why
    tbody
      tr
        td: code --rm
        td Removes the container when the tests end.
      tr
        td: code -v "$THUB_WORK_DIR:/work" -w /work
        td The checkout and downloads, and the #[code results/] and #[code artifacts/] folders, where the Client reads JUnit XML for the test counts.
      tr
        td: code -e NAME
        td Passes a job variable (#[code --env], #[code THUB_*], #[code JOB_*]) into the container.
      tr
        td: code --network host
        td Lets the container reach the DUT at #[code $THUB_DUT_HOST] (#[code 127.0.0.1:&lt;port&gt;]).
      tr
        td: code --device /dev/thub/dut1-uart
        td HW: gives the container the DUT's UART (#[code "$THUB_DUT_UART"]). Use #[code --privileged] only if you must.
      tr
        td: code --user "$(id -u):$(id -g)"
        td Files written to #[code /work] stay owned by the Client user, so the workspace can be cleaned up.
  +note('info').
    Docker access is effectively root access on that host. The Client host must have Docker and the service user must be in the
    #[code docker] group. The resource card's #[strong Capabilities] show whether a Client can use Docker, and why not.
